# S5720S-28TP-PWR-LI-ACL / V200R019C10SPC500
# 2026-10-14: REJECTED on this device. DO NOT DEPLOY this destination-exemption policy.
# GE0/0/4 inbound rejects it: IPv6 destination ip is not supported on current direction.
# Objects below are retained as a record of the failed candidate, not a working template.
# Remove failed interface binding: interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/4
# undo traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
# An alternative source-only policy requires edge hairpin-routing validation.
# Confirmed: IPv6 source /128 + link-local redirect works on VLAN1 inbound.
# Confirmed: VLAN inbound rejects IPv6 destination matching.
# Confirmed: IPv6 destination matching is also rejected on GE0/0/4 inbound.
# Internal destination exemptions and LOCAL precedence therefore cannot be validated here.
# Do not paste the complete file blindly. Sections below are separate stages.
# No running core configuration has been changed by creating this file.
# Check ACL 3980/3981/3982 and all object names are unused before creating.
# Current IPv6 prefixes are PD snapshots, not permanent allocations.
# User-provided VLAN inventory: VLAN1/2 both show GE0/0/2,4,6,7,8 UP/tagged.
# User identifies GE0/0/1 through GE0/0/6 as possible client ingress paths.
# This is a candidate range, not proof of the Mac's actual learned port.
# Resolve it with display mac-address; verify each interface before binding.
# GE0/0/7,8 must also be covered if they still carry VLAN1/2 client traffic.
# Other currently-down VLAN members require review before bringing them up.
# Eth-Trunk3 = GE0/0/23,24; Eth-Trunk4 = GE0/0/25,26, both LACP UP.
# Neither aggregate is listed in the supplied VLAN1/2 membership snapshots.
# Mac en0 now on VLAN2: IPv4 192.168.2.185, MAC 3ea3-f0ab-38ae.
# Core MAC table confirms 3ea3-f0ab-38ae in VLAN2 on GE0/0/4 (dynamic).
# User-supplied GE0/0/4 config: hybrid, PVID5; tagged VLAN1-4,6,100;
# untagged VLAN5; no existing inbound traffic-policy in this interface snapshot.
# Its existing VLAN membership, PoE and loopback-detect config need no change.
# Its current stable SLAAC IPv6 is used for the B /128 probe below.
# The A /128 is the earlier VLAN1 manual test address; recreate only on VLAN1.

# STAGE 1: Create UNBOUND objects. Internal destinations use permit, not deny.
# The /60 exemptions cover both current LAN/PD pools. They do not create routes.
# Additional internal/VPN IPv6 ranges outside these pools must be added here.
system-view
acl ipv6 3980
 rule 5 permit ipv6 destination 2408:8207:7866:a780:: 60
 rule 10 permit ipv6 destination 2408:8207:7865:78b0:: 60
 rule 15 permit ipv6 destination fc00:: 7
 rule 20 permit ipv6 destination fe80:: 10
 rule 25 permit ipv6 destination ff00:: 8
 quit
acl ipv6 3981
 rule 5 permit ipv6 source 2408:8207:7866:a785::c0de:1014 128
 quit
acl ipv6 3982
 rule 5 permit ipv6 source 2408:8207:7865:78b1:14b7:e9c6:ea47:e554 128
 quit
traffic classifier PBR6_LOCAL operator or
 if-match ipv6 acl 3980
 quit
traffic classifier PBR6_VLAN1 operator and
 if-match vlan-id 1
 if-match ipv6 acl 3981
 quit
traffic classifier PBR6_VLAN2 operator and
 if-match vlan-id 2
 if-match ipv6 acl 3982
 quit
traffic behavior PBR6_ROUTE
# Device emits: Info: CPCAR may be invalid if traffic behavior is configured as permit.
# This is a CPU-protection warning, not proof of application failure or success.
# Include CPU usage and ND/RA/OSPFv3 health in the single-port validation.
 permit
 statistics enable
 quit
traffic behavior PBR6_TO_A
 redirect ipv6-nexthop link-local FE80::B83B:17FF:FE74:32D0 interface Vlanif 3
 statistics enable
 quit
traffic behavior PBR6_TO_B
 redirect ipv6-nexthop link-local FE80::5434:4EFF:FEFA:68A4 interface Vlanif 4
 statistics enable
 quit
# Device CLI accepts only optional atomic here; match-order is not supported.
# Device CLI also accepts no precedence parameter in classifier/behavior binding.
# The creation order below is not proof of LOCAL override on this hardware.
# Global classifier help offers only operator and <cr>; no precedence parameter.
# Resolve priority semantics and verify overlapping LOCAL/egress before expansion.
traffic policy PBR6_EGRESS
 classifier PBR6_LOCAL behavior PBR6_ROUTE
 classifier PBR6_VLAN1 behavior PBR6_TO_A
 classifier PBR6_VLAN2 behavior PBR6_TO_B
 quit
return

# STAGE 2: Controlled /128 test on the verified Mac ingress GE0/0/4.
# Use the aggregate itself, not its member ports. Do not overwrite another policy.
# An existing IPv4 policy needs merging; never replace it with an IPv6-only policy.
# Record existing bindings first. Removal below concerns only our test policies.
# system-view
# vlan 1
#  undo traffic-policy PBR6_A_SRC_PROBE inbound
#  quit
# vlan 2
#  undo traffic-policy PBR6_B_SRC_PROBE inbound
#  quit
# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/4
#  traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# return
# display traffic-policy applied-record
# display traffic-policy statistics interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/4 inbound
# Test: VLAN1 -> A WAN; VLAN2 -> B WAN using the corresponding client /128.
# Test: VLAN1 <-> VLAN2 and VLAN1/2 -> VLAN3/4, using real hosts and TCP services.
# Test: the LOCAL counter rises; internal traffic is not redirected through edges.
# Re-read the Mac's address before testing: Wi-Fi/SLAAC addresses can change.
# If CLI syntax fails or any slot is fail, stop and restore the probe bindings.

# STAGE 3: Expand only after stage 2 passes, then bind ALL client ingress paths.
# Check actual current PD prefixes again before expansion.
# Candidate client-interface bindings AFTER each has been verified:
# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/1
#  traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/2
#  traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/3
#  traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/4
#  traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/5
#  traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/6
#  traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# GE0/0/5 is not listed in the supplied VLAN1/2 snapshots; confirm its VLAN config.
# Reconcile GE0/0/7,8 rather than silently dropping them from ingress coverage.
# system-view
# acl ipv6 3981
#  undo rule 5
#  rule 5 permit ipv6 source 2408:8207:7866:a785:: 64
#  quit
# acl ipv6 3982
#  undo rule 5
#  rule 5 permit ipv6 source 2408:8207:7865:78b1:: 64
#  quit
# return
# Recheck applied-record, both WAN paths, internal bidirectional TCP and ND/RA.
# Keep existing OSPFv3, VLANIF addresses, PD clients, route table and security ACLs.
# Enable VLAN1 RA only after confirming its actual ng3prefix/PIO is ready:
# system-view
# interface Vlanif 1
#  undo ipv6 nd ra halt
#  quit
# return
# save   <-- only after production validation passes

# ROLLBACK: Remove the new policy from EVERY interface where you applied it.
# For the single-interface stage 2 test, use GigabitEthernet 0/0/4 below.
# system-view
# interface <ACTUAL-INGRESS-INTERFACE>
#  undo traffic-policy PBR6_EGRESS inbound
#  quit
# vlan 1
#  traffic-policy PBR6_A_SRC_PROBE inbound
#  quit
# vlan 2
#  traffic-policy PBR6_B_SRC_PROBE inbound
#  quit
# return
# This restores the known probe state; it does not restore pre-probe production.
# If VLAN1 RA was enabled during this procedure and was originally halted:
# system-view
# interface Vlanif 1
#  ipv6 nd ra halt
#  quit
# return
